# Application-level Kconfig.
#
# Kconfig is Zephyr's build-time configuration language. Every `config` symbol
# here becomes a CONFIG_* macro your C code can test with #ifdef, and a value
# you can set in prj.conf or on the command line
# (-DCONFIG_CBPM_DONGLE_BLE=y). Kconfig decides what is COMPILED; devicetree
# decides what HARDWARE exists. Keep the two straight and Zephyr gets much
# easier.

menu "CBPM dongle"

config CBPM_DONGLE_BLE
	bool "Build the BLE central path (milestone M5)"
	default n
	select BT
	select BT_CENTRAL
	select BT_GATT_CLIENT
	select BT_GATT_DM
	help
	  Compiles src/ble/ble_central.c and pulls in the Bluetooth host and the
	  SoftDevice Controller. Leave this off until M5: the build is faster,
	  the image is smaller, and there is one less subsystem between you and
	  a bug. The `select` lines above are why you do not have to hand-write
	  the BT Kconfig options yourself -- turning this on turns those on too.

config CBPM_DONGLE_RING_SIZE
	int "Bytes in each direction of the bridge buffer"
	default 4096
	range 256 32768
	help
	  Size of the host-to-Roo and Roo-to-host ring buffers, in bytes. This is
	  the number that decides whether a burst is absorbed or dropped, so M4
	  asks you to reason about it rather than guess: at 1 Mbaud the wire
	  delivers 100 kB/s, and USB-CDC can stall for a millisecond or two at a
	  time. Work out how many bytes arrive in that window before you change
	  this.

config CBPM_DONGLE_STATUS_LED
	bool "Blink the on-board LED as a liveness sign"
	default y
	help
	  A heartbeat you can see without a debugger attached. Cheap, and the
	  first thing that tells you the scheduler is still running.

endmenu

# Every application Kconfig file must end by sourcing Zephyr's own, or none of
# the CONFIG_* symbols the kernel and drivers define would exist.
source "Kconfig.zephyr"
